{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words or Less\n\nI landed in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink confetti drifting over temple roofs and quiet streets. I didn’t plan much. I let the rhythm of Japan guide me.\n\nEach morning, I sipped matcha at a tiny café where the owner bowed without speaking. I walked the Philosopher’s Path, past moss-covered stone lanterns, listening to the whisper of water in the canal. At Nishiki Market, I tried tako tamago—octopus stuffed with quail egg—and didn’t regret it. (I did regret eating five taiyaki in one afternoon.)\n\nIn Tokyo, the city hummed like a symphony of efficiency. I got lost in Shinjuku’s neon glow, found a hidden izakaya where the chef grilled yakitori with quiet pride, and rode the Yamanote Line just to feel the pulse of the metropolis. No one pushed. No one yelled. Even in crowds, there was calm.\n\nI visited a ryokan in Hakone. The tatami floor was cool under bare feet. The kaiseki dinner was a poem on porcelain: sea urchin, simmered bamboo, a single persimmon. That night, I soaked in an onsen under stars, steam rising like breath against the cold air.\n\nThe most unexpected moment? Bowing to a stranger who dropped her umbrella. She bowed back, smiled, and handed me a single red candy from her pocket. No words exchanged. Just kindness, translated.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers—and if you’re quiet enough, you hear everything.\n\nI left with a suitcase full of tea, a notebook full of sketches, and a heart full of stillness. I didn’t just visit Japan. I remembered how to be present.\n\nNext time, I’ll stay longer. But for now, I carry the silence with me.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"to